I've written two task files and an issue-responses file. The plan isn't committed: session_plan/ is gitignored, so the plan commit did nothing. That's harmless, because the instructions' commit line ends with || true to allow for it.

Task 1 (product): multi-line edits on CRLF files
• **The bug:** this morning's test files (`/tmp/crlf`) showed that a multi-line edit on a Windows-style (CRLF) file always fails. The model writes the old text with plain \n, so it never matches the file's \r\n, and the model then gives the wrong reason for the failure. Single-line edits work.
• The fix: add one small function in src/smart_edit.rs. It runs only when the exact text isn't found but the CRLF version of it is, and then it writes the replacement with CRLF endings too. When a CRLF file still doesn't match, the error the model sees will say the file uses CRLF.
• **Things that must not change:**
  • LF files behave exactly as before.
  • Single-line edits behave exactly as before.
  • Old text that already contains \r\n never becomes \r\r\n.
• **Size limit:** smart_edit.rs is 1916 lines and the module-size test fails hard past 2050, so all new tests go in a new sibling file.

Task 2 (product, backlog drain): next piece of #869, `/cd` safety
• **The change:** after `/cd`, the new directory's `[directories] deny` entries also block the file tools. This only ever adds restrictions, so it follows the same pattern as Day 218's bash deny list.
• **What to watch for:** relative entries must be resolved against the `/cd` target when they're added, so a later `/cd` elsewhere doesn't shift them. The task also lists both places in tool_wrappers.rs that check paths.
• **Scope:** it fits in 3 source files and stays inside the line allowance for `tool_wrappers.rs` and `dispatch.rs`. The `/cd` notice wording changes in the same task so it doesn't claim the deny list is ignored.
• **Assessment correction:** this morning's assessment said /cd reloads nothing beyond the notice. That's out of date: the bash deny list has reloaded since Day 218.

